258
00:11:21,490 --> 00:11:24,730
My range isn't that
big, but I can come back

259
00:11:24,730 --> 00:11:27,450
and say, yeah, actually,
night is the most popular time

260
00:11:27,450 --> 00:11:30,350
for people to be
liking our post.

261
00:11:30,350 --> 00:11:30,850
Perfect.

262
00:11:35,050 --> 00:11:38,630
Now, how many impressions
happened on the weekend.

263
00:11:38,630 --> 00:11:40,930
So how many views
are we looking at?

264
00:11:40,930 --> 00:11:44,930
So I want to create a new
column called Weekend.

265
00:11:44,930 --> 00:11:47,080
And how would you
expect to do that?

266
00:11:47,080 --> 00:11:49,260
So Weekend,
Saturday, and Sunday.

267
00:11:49,260 --> 00:11:52,300
I have day of the
week data over here.

268
00:11:52,300 --> 00:11:53,620
So if I say--

269
00:11:53,620 --> 00:11:57,620
again, this goes back
to that AND or OR, which

270
00:11:57,620 --> 00:12:00,300
do you think I need to use?

271
00:12:00,300 --> 00:12:05,340
I need to use the OR because
I can't be both true Saturday

272
00:12:05,340 --> 00:12:07,240
and true on Sunday.

273
00:12:07,240 --> 00:12:09,620
It's only ever going to be
Saturday or Sunday in order

274
00:12:09,620 --> 00:12:10,912
for me to label it the weekend.

275
00:12:10,912 --> 00:12:13,860
And that's where this idea of if
comes into play really nicely.

276
00:12:13,860 --> 00:12:17,420
And what if does is it says,
I'm going to do this test.

277
00:12:17,420 --> 00:12:20,215
And if it's true, I'm
going to do something.

278
00:12:20,215 --> 00:12:22,340
And if it's false, I'm
going to do this other thing

279
00:12:22,340 --> 00:12:23,620
or just leave it blank.

280
00:12:23,620 --> 00:12:27,200
So let's first just see
if Saturday is true.

281
00:12:27,200 --> 00:12:34,940
So if this equals Saturday,
and I'm going to say "Weekend."

282
00:12:34,940 --> 00:12:38,520
Else, I'm going
to say "Weekday."

283
00:12:38,520 --> 00:12:39,200
And that works.

284
00:12:39,200 --> 00:12:42,042
First, remember, we're just
evaluating for Saturday.

285
00:12:42,042 --> 00:12:43,500
So right now, we're
labeling Sunday

286
00:12:43,500 --> 00:12:45,583
as a weekday, which we're
going to want to change.

287
00:12:45,583 --> 00:12:48,040
And we see that works
for Friday and Thursday.

288
00:12:48,040 --> 00:12:50,740
So based off of what we just
did for our morning, afternoon,

289
00:12:50,740 --> 00:12:52,793
evening, and night, go
ahead and make the change

290
00:12:52,793 --> 00:12:54,960
that you think you need in
order to consider if it's

291
00:12:54,960 --> 00:12:58,560
either Saturday or Sunday.

292
00:12:58,560 --> 00:13:00,782
So hopefully, when you
are working on that,

293
00:13:00,782 --> 00:13:02,740
we came to the conclusion
that we needed an OR.

294
00:13:02,740 --> 00:13:04,120
And remember, even though
we string it together

295
00:13:04,120 --> 00:13:05,495
in English in the
middle, we have

296
00:13:05,495 --> 00:13:09,320
to put it on the outside in
Excel for it to evaluate it.

297
00:13:09,320 --> 00:13:14,520
So OR, E2 is equal to Sunday.

298
00:13:14,520 --> 00:13:15,920
And then a close parentheses.

299
00:13:15,920 --> 00:13:18,480
So we're starting to nest
functions and formulas

300
00:13:18,480 --> 00:13:21,840
within each other, which is
pretty typical for Excel.

301
00:13:21,840 --> 00:13:25,100
So now let's do the same thing
and drag our formula down a few.

302
00:13:25,100 --> 00:13:28,320
And now we see that Sunday
is also considered a weekend.

303
00:13:28,320 --> 00:13:33,560
If we label this AND, everything
is going to actually be labeled

304
00:13:33,560 --> 00:13:37,280
"Weekday," because Excel doesn't
intuitively know that Saturday

305
00:13:37,280 --> 00:13:39,260
and Sunday is something
that we call weekend.

306
00:13:39,260 --> 00:13:41,800
We're just saying that
this set of characters

307
00:13:41,800 --> 00:13:43,970
has to be equal
to both Saturday.

308
00:13:43,970 --> 00:13:46,650
And since we're looking at
the same reference cell,

309
00:13:46,650 --> 00:13:49,030
it also has to be
equal to Sunday.

310
00:13:49,030 --> 00:13:52,290
If that's not true,
then it has to be false.

311
00:13:52,290 --> 00:13:54,610
And so that's why we're
getting weekday there

312
00:13:54,610 --> 00:14:01,330
and why we need OR as
our operator there.

313
00:14:01,330 --> 00:14:02,130
Perfect.

314
00:14:02,130 --> 00:14:07,830
So now that we have our
weekend column built out,

315
00:14:07,830 --> 00:14:09,990
how many impressions
happened on the weekend?

316
00:14:09,990 --> 00:14:10,490
OK.

317
00:14:10,490 --> 00:14:15,850
Go ahead and do
that calculation.

318
00:14:15,850 --> 00:14:16,470
All right.

319
00:14:16,470 --> 00:14:19,030
So now that you're
back, remember,

320
00:14:19,030 --> 00:14:23,130
we need to use the
COUNTIFs, because I

321
00:14:23,130 --> 00:14:25,770
need to use two different
conditions again.

322
00:14:25,770 --> 00:14:30,490
So first one is I want to
see if it's a weekend or not.

323
00:14:30,490 --> 00:14:36,350
Second, I want to see if my
event type is an impression.

324
00:14:36,350 --> 00:14:40,970
And so we see that on
weekends, we have about 96--

325
00:14:40,970 --> 00:14:43,460
almost 97,000 impressions
on a given weekend.

326
00:14:46,350 --> 00:14:47,650
Perfect!

327
00:14:47,650 --> 00:14:51,270
And now we'll go ahead
